Root cause identified and fixed in:

`firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/usart_irq/usart_irq.c`

### Root cause
USART2 was configured for **9600 baud** in firmware:

```c
usart_set_baudrate(USART2, 9600);
```

but your host communicates at **115200 baud**.  
That baud mismatch directly causes the garbled/unreadable bytes you observed.

### Fix applied
Updated USART2 initialization to 115200:

```c
usart_set_baudrate(USART2, 115200);
```

### Why this is the correct fix
For UART, both ends must match baud/format. With 9600 vs 115200, the receiver samples at the wrong time and reconstructs incorrect bits, so echo payload appears as random garbage instead of the sent byte.

### Verification note
A local build attempt in this environment is currently blocked by missing `libopencm3` path configuration (`OPENCM3_DIR` not set), but the code-level defect causing on-board garbled echo has been corrected.
